-- Q1: easy / L2 — FTC limitation formula application
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Redfield Corp has worldwide taxable income of $500,000, of which $200,000 is foreign-source. Its U.S. tax liability before credits is $105,000. Foreign taxes paid total $50,000. What is Redfield Corp's allowable foreign tax credit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$42,000", "$50,000", "$105,000", "$21,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 904(a), the FTC limitation equals U.S. tax liability multiplied by the ratio of foreign-source taxable income to worldwide taxable income. Here: $105,000 x ($200,000 / $500,000) = $42,000. The credit is the lesser of taxes paid ($50,000) or the limitation ($42,000). Wrong (B): This is the total foreign taxes paid, but the credit is capped at the limitation amount. Wrong (C): This equals the entire U.S. tax liability; the FTC cannot exceed the proportionate share attributable to foreign income. Wrong (D): This incorrectly uses a 10% rate rather than the statutory 21% rate applied through the limitation formula.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — GILTI computation with QBAI offset
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Westbrook Inc., a domestic C corporation, owns 100% of a CFC with tested income of $3,000,000. The CFC's qualified business asset investment (QBAI) is $12,000,000, and specified interest expense is $100,000. What is Westbrook's GILTI inclusion amount?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$3,000,000", "$1,900,000", "$1,800,000", "$2,100,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 951A, GILTI equals net CFC tested income minus net deemed tangible income return (DTIR). DTIR = 10% of QBAI minus specified interest expense = ($12,000,000 x 10%) - $100,000 = $1,100,000. GILTI = $3,000,000 - $1,100,000 = $1,900,000. Wrong (A): This fails to subtract the DTIR, treating all tested income as GILTI. Wrong (C): This omits the specified interest expense reduction from DTIR, overstating the offset. Wrong (D): This uses 5% of QBAI instead of the required 10%.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Subpart F foreign base company sales income
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Kensington Ltd., a CFC incorporated in Country X, purchases components from its U.S. parent and sells finished goods to customers in Country Y. The income from these sales is $800,000. How should this income be classified for U.S. tax purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Effectively connected income excluded from Subpart F", "Income exempt under the manufacturing exception", "Foreign base company sales income includible as Subpart F income", "Foreign-source income deferred until repatriation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 954(d), foreign base company sales income includes income from buying or selling property involving a related party where the CFC is in a different country than the property's origin or destination. Kensington buys from its U.S. parent and sells to Country Y customers—both outside Country X. Wrong (A): Effectively connected income applies to foreign persons with U.S. business activities, not CFC classification. Wrong (B): The manufacturing exception requires substantial transformation within the CFC's country; the facts indicate purchase and resale. Wrong (D): Subpart F income is included currently in the U.S. shareholder's income regardless of distribution.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Section 250 deduction effect on GILTI rate
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Thornton Corp, a domestic C corporation, has a GILTI inclusion of $2,000,000 for tax year 2026. Thornton has sufficient taxable income to claim the full Section 250 deduction. Before any foreign tax credits, what is Thornton's effective U.S. tax rate on its GILTI inclusion?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["21%", "10.5%", "16%", "13.125%"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 250(a), for tax years beginning after 2025, C corporations may deduct 37.5% of their GILTI inclusion. The effective rate is 21% x (1 - 0.375) = 21% x 0.625 = 13.125%. Wrong (A): This is the statutory corporate rate without applying the Section 250 deduction. Wrong (B): This was the effective GILTI rate for 2018-2025 when the Section 250 deduction was 50%. Wrong (C): This does not correspond to any correct application of the Section 250 deduction percentage.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — FTC basket classification
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Greystone Corp receives $150,000 in dividend income from a passive foreign investment and $400,000 in active branch profits from foreign manufacturing operations. For purposes of computing the foreign tax credit limitation, how must Greystone categorize this income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Dividends in the passive category basket and branch profits in the general category basket", "All income combined in a single basket", "Both items in the general category basket because they are corporate income", "Dividends in the general category basket and branch profits in the passive category basket"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 904(d), foreign tax credits are computed separately for two baskets. Passive investment dividends fall in the passive category basket, while active business income such as branch profits falls in the general category basket. Wrong (B): The Code requires separate basket calculations; combining all foreign income into one basket has not been permitted since the basket system was enacted. Wrong (C): The character of the income, not the taxpayer's entity type, determines basket classification. Wrong (D): This reverses the correct classification—passive dividends belong in the passive basket, not the general basket.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — BEAT vs regular tax comparison
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Magnus Corp has average annual gross receipts of $800 million and a base erosion percentage of 5%. Its regular tax liability is $18,000,000. Modified taxable income (computed without base erosion tax benefits) is $200,000,000. Which outcome results from the BEAT computation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Magnus owes no additional BEAT because its regular tax exceeds 10% of modified taxable income", "Magnus owes $2,000,000 in additional BEAT", "Magnus owes $20,000,000 as its total tax liability under BEAT", "Magnus is not subject to BEAT because its base erosion percentage is below 10%"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 59A, BEAT equals 10% of modified taxable income minus regular tax liability. Here: (10% x $200,000,000) - $18,000,000 = $20,000,000 - $18,000,000 = $2,000,000 additional tax. Magnus meets the applicable taxpayer thresholds ($500M+ gross receipts, 3%+ base erosion percentage). Wrong (A): The BEAT amount ($20M) exceeds regular tax ($18M), so the excess $2M is owed. Wrong (C): BEAT is the excess over regular tax, not a replacement of it. Total liability is $18M + $2M = $20M. Wrong (D): The base erosion percentage threshold is 3% (not 10%); Magnus's 5% exceeds this.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: hard / L3 — Transfer pricing penalty thresholds
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Apex Global Inc. failed to maintain contemporaneous documentation for intercompany transactions with its foreign subsidiaries. The IRS determined a Section 482 adjustment of $15,000,000, which constitutes a substantial valuation misstatement. What penalty applies to the underpayment attributable to this adjustment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No penalty if Apex demonstrates reasonable cause", "10% accuracy-related penalty", "20% accuracy-related penalty", "40% gross valuation misstatement penalty"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 6662(e), a substantial valuation misstatement triggers a 20% penalty on the underpayment. Without contemporaneous documentation under Sec. 6662(e)(3)(B), the taxpayer cannot establish reasonable cause as a defense. Wrong (A): The reasonable cause defense is unavailable when contemporaneous documentation was not maintained for transfer pricing adjustments. Wrong (B): The accuracy-related penalty for transfer pricing misstatements is 20%, not 10%. Wrong (D): The 40% penalty applies to gross valuation misstatements (400% or more of correct value), which requires a higher threshold than substantial misstatement.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — FDII computation and incentive evaluation
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Pinnacle Corp, a domestic C corporation, has deduction-eligible income (DEI) of $10,000,000, of which $6,000,000 is foreign-derived. QBAI is $20,000,000. Pinnacle is evaluating whether to serve foreign customers from its U.S. operations or through a new CFC. What is Pinnacle's FDII Section 250 deduction if it keeps operations domestic?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$750,000", "$1,125,000", "$2,250,000", "$1,800,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 250, DII = DEI - (10% x QBAI) = $10,000,000 - $2,000,000 = $8,000,000. Foreign-derived ratio = $6,000,000 / $10,000,000 = 60%. FDII = $8,000,000 x 60% = $4,800,000. Section 250 deduction = 37.5% x $4,800,000 = $1,800,000. Wrong (A): This incorrectly applies the deduction to total DEI without computing deemed intangible income. Wrong (B): This uses the old 50% deduction rate instead of the current 37.5% rate for post-2025 tax years. Wrong (C): This omits the QBAI offset, treating all foreign-derived DEI as FDII.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — FTC excess credit carryover application
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$In 2025, Hartwell Corp paid $120,000 in foreign taxes on general category income but its FTC limitation for that basket was $90,000. In 2026, Hartwell's general category FTC limitation is $140,000 and it pays $100,000 in current-year foreign taxes. How much total FTC can Hartwell claim in 2026 for the general category basket?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$130,000", "$140,000", "$120,000", "$100,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 904(c), excess foreign tax credits carry forward 10 years within the same basket. The 2025 excess is $120,000 - $90,000 = $30,000. In 2026, current taxes of $100,000 plus $30,000 carryforward = $130,000, which is within the $140,000 limitation. Wrong (B): The limitation is $140,000, but total available credits are only $130,000; the full limitation amount is not a credit. Wrong (C): This uses only the prior-year taxes paid without considering the current year's taxes and limitation. Wrong (D): This ignores the carryforward and claims only the current year's foreign taxes.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — CFC ownership aggregation test
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Four unrelated U.S. corporations each own 15% of the voting stock of Foreign Corp Z. No other shareholders are U.S. persons. The remaining 40% is owned by foreign individuals. Is Foreign Corp Z a controlled foreign corporation (CFC)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No, because no single U.S. shareholder owns more than 50%", "Yes, because the four U.S. shareholders collectively own more than 50%", "No, because each U.S. shareholder must own at least 25% to be counted", "Yes, but only if the U.S. shareholders have a formal voting agreement"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 957(a), a CFC is a foreign corporation in which U.S. shareholders (persons owning 10% or more of vote or value) collectively own more than 50% of total voting power or value. Each 15% owner qualifies as a U.S. shareholder, and their combined 60% exceeds the 50% threshold. Wrong (A): The 50% test is applied to aggregate ownership of all U.S. shareholders, not individual ownership. Wrong (C): The threshold for U.S. shareholder status is 10%, not 25%. Wrong (D): No formal agreement is required; the statute aggregates all U.S. shareholders' ownership regardless of coordination.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Cross-crediting prohibition between baskets
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Meridian Corp has excess foreign tax credits of $25,000 in the passive category basket and a limitation shortfall (unused limitation) of $30,000 in the general category basket. Meridian's tax advisor considers using the passive excess credits to offset the general category limitation. What is the correct treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The excess passive credits may offset the general category shortfall up to $25,000", "The excess passive credits may be reclassified as general category credits with IRS approval", "Cross-crediting is prohibited; the passive excess credits may only be carried forward within the passive basket", "Cross-crediting is allowed if both baskets contain the same type of foreign-source income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 904(d), the FTC limitation is computed separately for each basket. Cross-crediting between baskets is expressly prohibited. Excess credits in the passive basket cannot offset a limitation shortfall in the general basket; the $25,000 excess may only be carried forward within the passive basket. Wrong (A): Cross-crediting between baskets is prohibited by the separate basket limitation rules. Wrong (B): The IRS has no authority to reclassify credits between baskets; the statute mandates separate computation. Wrong (D): The income type within a basket does not create an exception to the separate basket requirement.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Definition of BEAT applicable taxpayer
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$What are the two threshold requirements for a corporation to be an "applicable taxpayer" subject to the Base Erosion and Anti-Abuse Tax (BEAT)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Gross receipts of $100 million+ and base erosion percentage of 5%+", "Gross receipts of $250 million+ and base erosion percentage of 10%+", "Gross receipts of $1 billion+ and base erosion percentage of 1%+", "Average annual gross receipts of $500 million+ over 3 years and base erosion percentage of 3%+"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 59A(e), an applicable taxpayer must have average annual gross receipts of at least $500 million over the 3-year period ending with the preceding tax year, and a base erosion percentage of 3% or more (2% for banks and securities dealers). Wrong (A): Both the gross receipts threshold and percentage are incorrect. Wrong (B): The gross receipts threshold is $500 million, not $250 million, and the percentage is 3%, not 10%. Wrong (C): The gross receipts threshold is $500 million (not $1 billion), and the percentage is 3% (not 1%).$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Arm's length standard definition
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$What standard does IRC Section 482 apply to transactions between related parties in a multinational group?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The arm's length standard", "The fair market value standard", "The book value standard", "The comparable profits standard"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Sec. 482 authorizes the IRS to reallocate income among related entities to clearly reflect income, requiring that related-party transactions produce results consistent with those between unrelated parties—the arm's length standard. Wrong (B): Fair market value is a valuation concept used in other tax contexts but is not the statutory standard under Section 482. Wrong (C): Book value reflects accounting cost basis and is not the transfer pricing standard. Wrong (D): Comparable profits method is one of several methods used to apply the arm's length standard, not the standard itself.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — GILTI vs FDII strategic evaluation
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Calloway Corp, a domestic C corporation, can serve European customers either through a new CFC or from its existing U.S. operations. Expected annual income from European sales is $5,000,000. The CFC would have no QBAI. Calloway's U.S. QBAI is $30,000,000 and total DEI is $20,000,000 (including the $5,000,000 if kept domestic). Ignoring foreign tax credits, which structure produces a lower effective U.S. tax rate on the European income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["CFC structure, because GILTI is taxed at 0%", "CFC structure, because GILTI with no QBAI produces a 13.125% effective rate versus a higher FDII effective rate on this income", "Both structures produce identical effective rates after Section 250 deductions", "CFC structure, because the Section 250 deduction is 50% for GILTI versus 37.5% for FDII"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GILTI with no CFC QBAI, the full $5M is GILTI. After the 37.5% Section 250 deduction, the effective rate is 13.125%. Under FDII, DII = $20M - (10% x $30M) = $17M. Foreign-derived ratio = $5M / $20M = 25%. FDII = $4.25M. Section 250 deduction = $1,593,750. The FDII effective rate on the $5M is higher than 13.125% because QBAI reduces DII before the foreign-derived ratio is applied. Wrong (A): GILTI is not taxed at 0%; it is included in income with a 37.5% deduction post-2025. Wrong (C): The two structures produce different effective rates due to QBAI offset mechanics differing between GILTI and FDII. Wrong (D): Post-2025, both GILTI and FDII receive a 37.5% Section 250 deduction; the 50% rate expired after 2025.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L2 — Subpart F current inclusion concept
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Barlow Corp, a U.S. corporation owning 100% of a CFC, receives no cash distributions during the year. The CFC earns $500,000 of foreign personal holding company income (dividends and interest). Must Barlow include any amount in its U.S. gross income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No, because no distribution was made", "No, because holding company income is exempt from U.S. taxation", "Yes, $500,000 as Subpart F income regardless of distribution", "Yes, but only 50% of the amount is includible"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 951(a), U.S. shareholders of a CFC must include their pro rata share of Subpart F income currently in gross income, regardless of whether any actual distribution is made. Foreign personal holding company income is a Subpart F category under Sec. 954(c). Wrong (A): Subpart F income is taxed currently on an inclusion basis, not upon distribution. Wrong (B): No such exemption exists; foreign personal holding company income is specifically listed as Subpart F income. Wrong (D): The full pro rata share is includible; there is no 50% reduction for Subpart F income.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Treaty permanent establishment and BEAT interaction
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$A foreign-parented multinational group has a U.S. subsidiary with $600 million average gross receipts and a 4% base erosion percentage. The subsidiary makes $30 million in deductible royalty payments to its foreign parent. The applicable tax treaty reduces withholding on royalties to 0%. The subsidiary's CFO argues that the treaty-reduced withholding eliminates any U.S. anti-abuse concern. Which analysis is most accurate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The CFO is correct; treaty benefits override BEAT", "BEAT does not apply because the subsidiary is foreign-parented", "The royalty payments are excluded from base erosion payments under the treaty", "The CFO is incorrect; BEAT applies independently of treaty withholding rates because it targets deductible payments, not withholding"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 59A, BEAT operates on the deduction side, targeting base erosion payments that reduce taxable income. Treaty withholding rates address a different mechanism (source-country tax on outbound payments). The royalty deductions are base erosion payments regardless of the withholding rate. Wrong (A): BEAT is a statutory provision that applies independently of treaty withholding reductions; treaties do not override BEAT. Wrong (B): BEAT applies to U.S. corporations meeting the threshold requirements regardless of whether the parent is foreign or domestic. Wrong (C): Treaty provisions do not exclude payments from the BEAT computation; BEAT was specifically designed to address base erosion through deductible payments.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Insurance income as Subpart F
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Oakmont Corp owns a CFC incorporated in Country P that provides property insurance to unrelated customers. Of the CFC's total premiums, 70% cover risks located within Country P and 30% cover risks in Countries Q and R. Which portion, if any, constitutes Subpart F insurance income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The 30% covering risks outside Country P", "The entire 100% of premium income", "None, because the customers are unrelated parties", "The 70% covering risks inside Country P"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 953(a), Subpart F insurance income includes income from insuring risks located outside the CFC's country of organization. Only the 30% of premiums covering risks in Countries Q and R qualifies. The 70% covering domestic Country P risks is excluded. Wrong (B): The entire amount would be Subpart F income only if all risks were outside Country P. Wrong (C): The related-party requirement applies to foreign base company sales and services income, not insurance income; insurance income is based on risk location. Wrong (D): Income from insuring risks within the CFC's own country is excluded from Subpart F.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L2 — FTC carryback and carryforward periods
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Nelson Corp paid $80,000 in foreign taxes but its FTC limitation for the year is $60,000. Nelson claims the maximum allowable credit of $60,000. What options does Nelson have for the $20,000 excess credit?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deduct it as an expense in the current year only", "Carry it back one year and forward ten years", "Carry it forward five years only", "The excess is permanently lost"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 904(c), excess foreign tax credits may be carried back one year and carried forward ten years within the same basket. Alternatively, the taxpayer could elect to deduct the full amount rather than credit it. Wrong (A): A deduction is an alternative to claiming the credit, not a remedy for excess credits. Wrong (C): The carryforward period is ten years, not five, and a one-year carryback is also available. Wrong (D): Excess credits are not permanently lost; the carryback and carryforward provisions preserve their benefit.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Base erosion payments definition
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Sterling Corp, a U.S. corporation meeting BEAT thresholds, makes the following payments to its foreign parent during the year: $5 million in deductible interest, $3 million in deductible management fees, and $2 million in cost of goods sold. Which payments are classified as base erosion payments?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Interest only", "All three payments", "Interest and management fees only", "Cost of goods sold only"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 59A(d), base erosion payments include deductible amounts paid or accrued to foreign related parties, such as interest, royalties, and service fees. Cost of goods sold is generally excluded from base erosion payments. Therefore, only the $5 million interest and $3 million management fees qualify. Wrong (A): Management fees for services are also base erosion payments under Sec. 59A(d), not just interest. Wrong (B): Cost of goods sold is excluded from base erosion payments under the statute. Wrong (D): COGS is specifically excluded; interest and service fees are the base erosion payments.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Transfer pricing method selection evaluation
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Vertex Corp sells finished goods to its wholly-owned foreign subsidiary at a transfer price. Comparable uncontrolled transactions exist in the market, but the foreign subsidiary adds significant intangible value through local marketing and distribution. Vertex's tax advisor must select the most reliable transfer pricing method. Which method is most appropriate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Comparable uncontrolled price (CUP) method", "Cost plus method", "Resale price method", "Profit split method"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 482 and Treas. Reg. 1.482-6, when both parties contribute significant intangible value, the profit split method is most appropriate because it allocates combined profits based on each party's relative contributions. Wrong (A): CUP requires highly comparable transactions; when the subsidiary adds significant intangible value, the transactions differ materially from comparables. Wrong (B): Cost plus is best suited when the tested party performs routine manufacturing without significant intangible contributions. Wrong (C): Resale price works best when the reseller adds no significant value through intangibles; here the subsidiary contributes marketing intangibles.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Treaty reduced withholding rates
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$A U.S. corporation pays $1,000,000 in dividends to its 25%-owner foreign parent corporation. Without a treaty, the statutory U.S. withholding rate on dividends is 30%. The applicable tax treaty reduces the rate to 5% for direct investments of 10% or more. What is the U.S. withholding tax on this dividend?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$50,000", "$150,000", "$300,000", "$100,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the applicable treaty, the withholding rate on dividends paid to a parent owning 10% or more is reduced to 5%. Withholding = $1,000,000 x 5% = $50,000. Wrong (B): This applies a 15% rate, which is the typical treaty rate for portfolio dividends (less than 10% ownership), not direct investment dividends. Wrong (C): This applies the statutory 30% rate without considering treaty benefits. Wrong (D): This applies a 10% rate, which is not the standard treaty-reduced rate for direct investment dividends.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Subpart F country-of-incorporation exclusion
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Delta Corp owns a CFC incorporated in Country M. The CFC purchases goods from Delta Corp (U.S.) and resells them to unrelated customers located within Country M. Is the CFC's resale income classified as foreign base company sales income under Subpart F?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because the CFC purchased from a related U.S. party", "No, because the goods are sold for use within the CFC's country of incorporation", "Yes, because the CFC did not manufacture the goods", "No, because the customers are unrelated parties"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 954(d), foreign base company sales income requires that the property be manufactured, produced, or sold for use outside the CFC's country of incorporation. Since the goods are sold to customers within Country M (the CFC's country), the income does not meet this requirement. Wrong (A): A related-party purchase alone is insufficient; the destination must also be outside the CFC's country. Wrong (C): Manufacturing status is relevant only for the branch rule and same-country exception, not the basic classification. Wrong (D): The related-party test applies to the buying or selling side, not the customer relationship for the destination test.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — GILTI and FDII as complementary framework
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$A policy analyst explains that GILTI and FDII were designed to work together as a unified framework. A domestic C corporation earns $4,000,000 serving foreign markets from the U.S. and separately has a CFC earning $4,000,000 abroad with minimal tangible assets. Both amounts receive a 37.5% Section 250 deduction. What is the primary policy distinction between the GILTI and FDII provisions?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["GILTI applies only to passive income while FDII applies to active income", "FDII applies only to S corporations while GILTI applies to C corporations", "GILTI penalizes shifting profits offshore while FDII rewards keeping operations onshore", "GILTI and FDII are alternative elections; a corporation must choose one"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): GILTI imposes a minimum tax on CFC earnings exceeding a routine return on tangible assets, discouraging offshore profit shifting. FDII provides a preferential rate for domestic income derived from serving foreign markets, incentivizing onshore operations. Together they create symmetric incentives. Wrong (A): GILTI applies to tested income broadly, including active business income, not just passive income. Wrong (B): Both FDII and GILTI Section 250 deductions are available to C corporations. Individual CFC shareholders may elect Section 962 treatment for GILTI. Wrong (D): They are not alternative elections; both can apply simultaneously to different income streams of the same corporation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Section 962 election for individual CFC shareholders
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Patterson, an individual U.S. shareholder, owns 100% of a CFC with $1,000,000 of GILTI inclusion. Without a Section 962 election, Patterson's GILTI would be taxed at individual rates (up to 37%). Patterson's tax advisor recommends a Section 962 election. What is the primary benefit of this election?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It eliminates the GILTI inclusion entirely", "It defers taxation until the CFC distributes earnings", "It converts ordinary GILTI income into long-term capital gain", "It allows Patterson to be taxed at corporate rates and claim the Section 250 deduction and foreign tax credits"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 962, an individual electing corporate treatment is taxed on CFC inclusions as if the individual were a C corporation, gaining access to the 21% corporate rate, the Section 250 deduction (reducing the effective rate to 13.125%), and deemed-paid foreign tax credits. Wrong (A): Section 962 does not eliminate the inclusion; it changes the applicable rate and available deductions. Wrong (B): GILTI must be included currently regardless of election; Section 962 does not create deferral. Wrong (C): The election does not recharacterize the income as capital gain; it applies corporate rates and deductions to ordinary GILTI income.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Definition of CFC
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$What ownership threshold must U.S. shareholders collectively exceed for a foreign corporation to be classified as a controlled foreign corporation (CFC)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["More than 50% of total combined voting power or value", "At least 80% of total combined voting power", "More than 25% of total combined voting power", "At least 10% of total combined voting power or value"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 957(a), a CFC is any foreign corporation in which U.S. shareholders (those owning 10% or more of vote or value) own more than 50% of total combined voting power or value of stock. Wrong (B): The 80% threshold applies to affiliated group membership for consolidated return purposes, not CFC classification. Wrong (C): The 25% threshold is not used in CFC determination. Wrong (D): The 10% threshold defines a U.S. shareholder, not the aggregate ownership needed for CFC status.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Definition of permanent establishment
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$In the context of international tax treaties, what is a permanent establishment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A U.S. subsidiary of a foreign corporation", "A fixed place of business that creates taxing jurisdiction in a country", "Any sales activity conducted in a foreign country", "A branch office that has been operating for more than one year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under most bilateral tax treaties, a permanent establishment is a fixed place of business through which an enterprise carries on its business, creating taxing jurisdiction for the host country. Without a PE, treaty protection generally prevents the source country from taxing business profits. Wrong (A): A subsidiary is a separate legal entity, not a PE; PE refers to a branch or fixed place of business. Wrong (C): Mere sales activity without a fixed place of business does not necessarily create a PE. Wrong (D): Duration alone does not define a PE; the concept requires a fixed place of business, though some treaties include time thresholds for specific activities.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Foreign personal holding company income categories
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Which of the following is classified as foreign personal holding company income under the Subpart F rules?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Active manufacturing income earned in the CFC's country", "Income from services performed in the CFC's country", "Dividends, interest, rents, and royalties", "Sales income from goods manufactured by the CFC"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 954(c), foreign personal holding company income includes dividends, interest, rents, royalties, and net gains from property transactions. This is a category of Subpart F income subject to current inclusion by U.S. shareholders. Wrong (A): Active manufacturing income earned within the CFC's country is generally not Subpart F income. Wrong (B): Services income earned within the CFC's country of incorporation is excluded from foreign base company services income. Wrong (D): Sales income from goods the CFC manufactures itself is generally excluded from foreign base company sales income.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — BEAT minimum tax nature
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$How does the Base Erosion and Anti-Abuse Tax (BEAT) function in relation to a corporation's regular income tax liability?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It replaces the regular income tax entirely", "It applies only to foreign-source income", "It reduces the corporation's regular tax liability through credits", "It imposes additional tax only when the BEAT amount exceeds the regular tax liability"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 59A, the BEAT operates as a minimum tax. If 10% of modified taxable income exceeds the corporation's regular tax liability, the excess is an additional tax. If regular tax already exceeds the BEAT amount, no additional tax is owed. Wrong (A): BEAT supplements regular tax; it does not replace it. Wrong (B): BEAT applies to modified taxable income, which includes both domestic and foreign-source income. Wrong (C): BEAT imposes additional tax rather than providing credits; it actually limits the use of certain credits in the modified taxable income computation.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Transfer pricing contemporaneous documentation
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Regal Industries conducts $50 million in annual intercompany transactions with its foreign subsidiaries. Regal's tax department prepares transfer pricing documentation after year-end but before the tax return filing date. The IRS proposes a Section 482 adjustment and asserts that Regal lacks contemporaneous documentation. Which consequence follows?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Regal cannot assert a reasonable cause defense against the accuracy-related penalty", "The IRS adjustment is automatically upheld without further analysis", "Regal faces criminal penalties for transfer pricing noncompliance", "The documentation prepared before filing is deemed contemporaneous"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 6662(e)(3)(B) and Treas. Reg. 1.6662-6(d), contemporaneous documentation must exist at the time the return is filed. Without it, the taxpayer cannot establish reasonable cause as a defense to the 20% or 40% accuracy-related penalty for transfer pricing misstatements. Wrong (B): The IRS must still demonstrate that the taxpayer's pricing does not satisfy the arm's length standard; lack of documentation does not create an automatic adjustment. Wrong (C): Transfer pricing penalties under Section 482 are civil, not criminal. Wrong (D): Documentation must exist when the return is filed; post-year-end preparation may not meet the contemporaneous standard under the regulations.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Treaty override last-in-time rule
(6, 'Multi-Jurisdictional Tax Planning', $EXPL$Congress enacts a new statute in 2026 that imposes a 15% minimum withholding tax on certain cross-border payments. An existing tax treaty with Country K provides a 5% withholding rate on those same payments. A taxpayer subject to the treaty argues the treaty rate should apply. Under U.S. law, which provision controls?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The treaty always controls because treaties are the supreme law of the land", "The later-enacted statute controls under the last-in-time rule", "The taxpayer may elect the more favorable provision", "The IRS must renegotiate the treaty before applying the new statute"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under U.S. constitutional law, treaties and federal statutes have equal status. When a later-enacted statute conflicts with a prior treaty, the statute prevails under the last-in-time rule. The 2026 statute would override the treaty's 5% rate. Wrong (A): While treaties are the supreme law under Article VI, they share equal rank with federal statutes. A later statute can override a prior treaty. Wrong (C): There is no taxpayer election between conflicting treaty and statutory provisions; the last-in-time rule determines which controls. Wrong (D): Renegotiation is not required; Congress has the constitutional authority to override treaties through subsequent legislation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
